, 'note_tweet': {'is_expandable': True, 'note_tweet_results': {'result': {'id': 'Tm90ZVR3ZWV0OjE4Nzc0NTA5NzA2NDIzOTUxNDA=', 'text': 'Cuomo is engaging in pure sophistry.\n\nAnalogy: If “consequences” such as arrest and imprisonment exist for armed robbery, we can conclude there is no “free choice” of armed robbery, which will be (or should be) thwarted by coercion (i.e. arrest and prosecution) if possible. Yes, it’s possible to choose to rob a store, but trying to deprive people of life or property is not deemed a “freedom” in any reasonable sense, at least not in any society where the rule of law has a modicum of existence.\n\nBy the same token, it is ludicrous to suggest that people threatened with losing their livelihoods if they don’t get vaccinated have a “free choice.” It is coercion, just as arresting an armed robbery suspect is coercion. (Most of us would deem the latter justified coercion, i.e. a means of protecting society from harm.)\n\nIf you think people should be arrested before they can commit a planned armed robbery, you are advocating a form of coercion (which, again, most of us would consider justified). If you think people should be fired from their jobs or forbidden from leaving their homes for not getting vaccinated, you are advocating a form of coercion, which, presumably, you consider necessary to protect society. It is intellectually and morally bankrupt to pretend otherwise. If you are going to adopt this position, please at least own it. \n\nThis is a connected but separate issue from whether you think the vaccine was effective, dangerous, or whatnot. People who support Trump can still disagree with him on vaccine-related matters. “You voted for him” is an irrelevant point unless the vaccine was the only issue people voted on.\n\n(This is also somewhat akin to the “freedom of speech isn’t freedom of reach” argument, which is analogous to telling someone, “OK, say whatever you want” just before locking them inside a soundproof room.)', 'enti